PLACEHOLDER — replace this file with your module's licence text.

The generated `Directory.Build.props` declares
`<PackageLicenseExpression>PACKAGE_LICENSE</PackageLicenseExpression>`, so the package metadata
already claims a licence. This file is what a reader of the repository sees, and what
`--license` selected must actually be.

Two things to do before the first `Pack`:

1. Replace the whole of this file with the full text of the licence you chose. For Apache-2.0
   that is the text published at https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt; for MIT it is
   the short template with your copyright line filled in.
2. Check that `PackageLicenseExpression` in `Directory.Build.props` names the same licence, as an
   SPDX identifier (`Apache-2.0`, `MIT`, `BSD-3-Clause`, …). A mismatch between the two is worse
   than either being wrong on its own: consumers read the metadata, auditors read this file.

If the module is proprietary and not to be licensed at all, remove `PackageLicenseExpression`
from `Directory.Build.props` and state the terms here instead — NuGet will warn about the missing
expression, which is the correct signal for a package that has none.
